-- title:
--   The closed cube at the step index contains the point
-- statement:
--   Fix a side length $h > 0$, and write $\|x\|_\infty = \max_i |x_i|$ for the maximum norm on $\mathbb{R}^d$. For a point $s \in \mathbb{R}^d$ let
--   $$x_h(s) \;=\; \bigl(\,\operatorname{round}(s_i/h)\cdot h\,\bigr)_{i} \qquad (\texttt{stepIndex d h s})$$
--   be the nearest point of the scaled lattice $h\mathbb{Z}^d$, coordinate by coordinate — equivalently the lattice point of the unique half-closed cube $\prod_i [\,u_i - h/2,\ u_i + h/2)$, $u \in h\mathbb{Z}^d$, containing $s$ — and let
--   $$\bar A_h(u) \;=\; \{\, x \in \mathbb{R}^d : \|x - u\|_\infty \le h/2 \,\} \qquad (\texttt{closedCube h u})$$
--   be the closed cube of side $h$ centred at $u$. The claim is that
--   $$s \in \bar A_h\bigl(x_h(s)\bigr)$$
--   for every $s \in \mathbb{R}^d$.
--
--   The step index is the device that turns a point into a discrete label, and this lemma says the label is honest: the point really lies in the cube its index names. Every estimate that compares the value of a function at $s$ with its average over the tile of $s$ passes through this membership, since it is what bounds $\|s - x_h(s)\|$ by the diameter of a cube of side $h$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Positivity of $h$ is required: for $h \le 0$ the tiling is degenerate. The closed cube (with the non-strict inequality $\|x-u\|_\infty \le h/2$) is used here rather than the half-closed tile, because the closed version is the one appearing in the Vitali-family and averaging estimates; the half-closed counterpart is stated separately.
